{"id":"overview-working-with-objects-common-labels","corpus":"k8s","title":"Recommended Labels","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/common-labels/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Recommended Labels"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can visualize and manage Kubernetes objects with more tools than kubectl and the dashboard. A common set of labels allows tools to work interoperably, describing objects in a common manner that all tools can understand."},{"kind":"p","text":"In addition to supporting tooling, the recommended labels describe applications in a way that can be queried."},{"kind":"p","text":"The metadata is organized around the concept of an application. Kubernetes is not a platform as a service (PaaS) and doesn't have or enforce a formal notion of an application. Instead, applications are informal and described with metadata. The definition of what an application contains is loose."},{"kind":"p","text":"These are recommended labels. They make it easier to manage applications but aren't required for any core tooling."},{"kind":"p","text":"Shared labels and annotations share a common prefix: app.kubernetes.io. Labels without a prefix are private to users. The shared prefix ensures that shared labels do not interfere with custom user labels."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Labels"},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to take full advantage of using these labels, they should be applied on every resource object."},{"kind":"p","text":"To illustrate these labels in action, consider the following StatefulSet object:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Applications And Instances Of Applications"},{"kind":"p","text":"An application can be installed one or more times into a Kubernetes cluster and, in some cases, the same namespace. For example, WordPress can be installed more than once where different websites are different installations of WordPress."},{"kind":"p","text":"The name of an application and the instance name are recorded separately. For example, WordPress has a app.kubernetes.io/name of wordpress while it has an instance name, represented as app.kubernetes.io/instance with a value of wordpress-abcxyz. This enables the application and instance of the application to be identifiable. Every instance of an application must have a unique name."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Examples"},{"kind":"p","text":"To illustrate different ways to use these labels the following examples have varying complexity."},{"kind":"h3","text":"A Simple Stateless Service"},{"kind":"p","text":"Consider the case for a simple stateless service deployed using Deployment and Service objects. The following two snippets represent how the labels could be used in their simplest form."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Deployment is used to oversee the pods running the application itself."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Service is used to expose the application."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Web Application With A Database"},{"kind":"p","text":"Consider a slightly more complicated application: a web application (WordPress) using a database (MySQL), installed using Helm. The following snippets illustrate the start of objects used to deploy this application."},{"kind":"p","text":"The start to the following Deployment is used for WordPress:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Service is used to expose WordPress:"},{"kind":"p","text":"MySQL is exposed as a StatefulSet with metadata for both it and the larger application it belongs to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Service is used to expose MySQL as part of WordPress:"},{"kind":"p","text":"With the MySQL StatefulSet and Service you'll notice information about both MySQL and WordPress, the broader application, are included."}],"lemmas":{"recommended":["recommended","recommend"],"recommendeds":["recommendeds","recommended"],"recommends":["recommends","recommend"],"recommending":["recommending","recommend"],"la